-- Pair: product type (two-element tuple), with the CANONICAL equality and ordering of
-- a product — both componentwise, both provided by `Pair` itself.
--
-- ── THE ORDER IS LEXICOGRAPHIC, fst-THEN-snd, AND IT IS CANONICAL ────────────────
--
-- That is the order every neighbouring language gives a pair (Haskell's `Ord (a, b)`,
-- Rust's `Ord for (A, B)`, Python tuples, `std::pair`), so `Pair` provides it ITSELF
-- rather than shipping it as a named witness beside a rival. The consequence is the
-- point: a bracket-less `Ordered.compare` on a `Pair` ANSWERS — one provider, tier 2 —
-- and `SortedSet.empty[T = Pair[Int64, Int64]]()` needs no ordering bracket at all.
-- Before this, nothing in the prelude ordered a `Pair`, so pairs could not be sorted.
--
-- AN ALTERNATIVE ORDERING IS THE PROGRAM'S TO DECLARE, not the library's to impose.
-- A witness bundling its own `PartialOrd` + `Ordered` over `Pair[A, B]` coexists with
-- this provision and is selected by name (`SortedSet.empty[…, O = MyOrder]()`) — the
-- 058 §3.2 tier-3 machinery, exercised in `wi858_pair_orderings_test`. Shipping such a
-- rival HERE would have made every downstream bracket-less compare on a pair an error
-- its author never opted into, which is the same reason no prelude witness may order
-- `String` (`docs/brainstorms/prelude-multiple-orderings.md`, obstacle A).
--
-- ONE MEASURED LIMIT OF THAT COEXISTENCE, and it is the ladder's, not this file's:
-- while a rival is declared, the CANONICAL order becomes unreachable through a
-- `requires` slot. `Pair` is a CONCRETE provider (its values carry their own sort), so
-- 058 §3.5 check 3 refuses an explicit `[Ordered = Pair]` — the value decides — while
-- the rival makes the bracket-less goal ambiguous. So such a program can bracket the
-- rival but cannot ask for the canonical one back. Rung 2a (058 §3.2, WI-861) is
-- exactly the missing rung: the carrier's own provision is the INFERRED default, so
-- silence takes `Pair` and the rival stays opt-in — with no edit to this file.
--
-- ── EQUALITY IS COMPONENTWISE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- The `requires` clauses below do double duty: they CONDITION the provisions and they
-- are the EVIDENCE the `eq` / `compare` bodies dispatch through. That is the `Set`
-- precedent — `set.anthill`'s sort-level `requires Eq[T]`, honoured through the
-- `provides` fact rather than op-scoped — applied to a two-parameter carrier.
--
-- THE EQUALITY CHAIN IS `PartialEq`, NOT `Eq`, deliberately: with an `Eq` chain,
-- `Pair[A = Float, B = Int64]` became a LOAD ERROR (MEASURED) — `Float` provides
-- `NonEq`, and WI-835's use-site check refuses a `NonEq` carrier at a parameter whose
-- sort `requires Eq` — i.e. `Pair` stopped being a general PRODUCT. `Set`/`Map`
-- genuinely need `Eq` on keys; a pair of anything is a pair.
--
-- TWO OVER-CLAIMS RIDE THAT DECISION, recorded rather than hidden, because a sort has
-- ONE `requires` chain shared by every provision it makes:
--
--   * `provides Eq[Pair]` rides the `PartialEq` chain, so it claims LAWFUL equality
--     even where a component has only the partial one. MEASURED consequence:
--     `Set[T = Pair[Float, Int64]]` loads while `Set[T = Float]` is refused — a NaN
--     reaches a lawful-key position inside a pair. (That gap PREDATES these provisions
--     — measured on the pre-provision `Pair`, which provided neither `Eq` nor `NonEq`
--     and so was simply *unconstrained*; what changed is that the claim is now
--     positively asserted rather than absent. `eq.anthill`'s own note calls the
--     congruent `NonEq` derivation for composites a follow-up.)
--   * `requires Ordered[A], Ordered[B]` conditions the ORDERING but sits on that same
--     shared chain, so every `Pair` nominally demands ordered components — false for
--     `Pair[A = Term, B = Term]` and its kin, and unenforced (no use-site check exists
--     for `Ordered`, unlike `Eq`'s). Those dictionary slots simply go unresolved, and
--     nothing reads them.
--
-- Both are one fix: per-provision conditions (058 §3.8's `provides X[…] :- goals`),
-- which would spell `provides Eq[Pair] :- Eq[A], Eq[B]` beside
-- `provides Ordered[Pair] :- Ordered[A], Ordered[B]`. WI-869.
--
-- ONE MORE MEASURED LIMIT, and it is not this file's defect: a NESTED pair whose FIRST
-- component is a `Pair` and whose second is a primitive
-- (`eq(pair(fst: pair(fst: 1, snd: 2), snd: 7), …)`) dies at eval, the second
-- component's compare dispatching to `Pair.eq`. PRE-EXISTING — reproduced on a purely
-- local carrier with no prelude involvement, and `A = Int64, B = <carrier>` passes —
-- but it is this file that makes it reachable from the standard library. WI-871, and
-- pinned in `wi858_pair_orderings_test` so it is not discovered twice.
enum anthill.prelude.Pair
  import anthill.prelude.{Bool, Int64, PartialEq, Eq, Ordered, PartialOrd}
  import anthill.prelude.PartialOrd.{gte, lte}

  sort A = ?
  sort B = ?
  requires PartialEq[A]
  requires PartialEq[B]
  requires Ordered[A]
  requires Ordered[B]
  entity pair(fst: A, snd: B)

  provides PartialEq[Pair]
  provides Eq[Pair]
  -- The BUNDLE (058 §3.8): `ordered.anthill` derives `gt`/`lt`/`gte`/`lte` from
  -- `compare` off the carrier's `PartialOrd`, so a provider of `Ordered` must supply
  -- the `PartialOrd` those derivations read. Supplying one floor over a foreign other
  -- is what makes a lone `Ordered` witness unlawful.
  provides PartialOrd[Pair]
  provides Ordered[Pair]

  operation fst(p: Pair) -> A
  operation snd(p: Pair) -> B
  -- TODO (WI-324 follow-up): add operation bodies. Both `match p case pair(a, _) -> a`
  -- and `p.fst` currently fail the typer with "expected A, got Term" — the entity-field
  -- sort doesn't propagate through self-typed match/access in concrete enum sorts. The
  -- rules below remain as the de-facto implementation until the typer issue is fixed.
  rule fst(pair(?a, ?b)) <=> ?a
  rule snd(pair(?a, ?b)) <=> ?b

  -- Component compares go through the SEMANTIC spec ops, not through structural
  -- unification, so both dispatch all the way down — a pair of `Set`s compares its
  -- component by MEMBERSHIP. Each call resolves through the matching `requires` slot.
  operation eq(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Bool =
    match a
      case pair(al, ar) ->
        match b
          case pair(bl, br) ->
            if PartialEq.eq(al, bl) then PartialEq.eq(ar, br) else false

  -- Lexicographic. The second component decides only on a TIE, so the only question
  -- the first compare's result has to answer is whether it is 0 — a three-way
  -- `lt`/`gt` split would return `c` from two of its arms.
  operation compare(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Int64 =
    match a
      case pair(al, ar) ->
        match b
          case pair(bl, br) ->
            let c = Ordered.compare(al, bl)
            if PartialEq.eq(c, 0) then Ordered.compare(ar, br) else c

  -- `Ordered` derives `max`/`min` by rule from `compare`, but a PROVIDER must still
  -- back them (WI-818: a rule is not backing), so they are spelled here.
  operation max(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Pair = if gte(compare(a, b), 0) then a else b
  operation min(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Pair = if lte(compare(a, b), 0) then a else b
end
